Emerging Powers
What Are Emerging Powers?
Emerging powers are nations that are experiencing significant economic growth, expanding
political influence, and increasingly playing more prominent roles in regional and global
affairs. They may increasingly be considered great powers as a result of this growth.
Globalisation has driven the emergence of many of these powers, many of which have sizeable
populations and are turning this into economic, military and diplomatic might as they industrialise
and modernise. Though they may not yet possess the full spectrum of influence enjoyed by
established powers, emerging powers are key players in shaping the future global order.
As will be explored below, the BRICS used to be considered the main ‘emerging’ powers, however
this is arguably now not too useful as will be explored below. Though there is of course significant
debate, some of the most significant current emerging powers are: India, Brazil, Indonesia, Nigeria,
Turkey, Mexico and potentially Saudi Arabia and Iran.
The BRICS
The term BRICs—referring to Brazil, Russia, India, and China—was coined in the early
2000s to describe a group of rapidly growing economies that were expected to significantly
influence global economic and political affairs. These countries were characterised by their
large populations, abundant natural resources, and fast-growing economies, which allowed them to
play an increasingly important role in global markets and international diplomacy.
The BRICS group formalised their cooperation in 2009, holding their first official summit in
Yekaterinburg, Russia. This marked the transition from an informal economic concept to a cohesive
political and economic bloc. In 2010, South Africa was invited to join, officially transforming BRIC
into BRICS. Since then, the group has met annually at summits to discuss global governance,
economic cooperation, and development strategies. In addition to the summits, BRICS members
have established key institutions like the New Development Bank (NDB), aimed at funding
infrastructure and development projects in emerging economies, further cementing their role in the
global political and economic landscape
https://www.politicsexplained.co.uk/ 6
Though their cooperation and formation as a group separate from US influence is geopolitically
significant, using them as a shorthand for the current emerging powers is no longer useful for a
number of reasons:
1. Russia shouldn’t be considered ‘emerging’ due to its historical power.
2. China is now considered by many to be a superpower and is far more powerful than the
other members of the BRICS.
3. South Africa in particular has been overtaken by many other powers.
4. A number of other emerging powers have grown in importance, including Indonesia, Turkey
and Nigeria.
Case Study 1: India
India has long been considered an emerging power and is now seen by many as a great power,
largely due to its vast population, economic growth, and increasing international influence.
With a population of over 1.4 billion, India is the most populous country in the world, and its
economy is the fifth-largest, with a nominal GDP of about $3.4 trillion (2024). India has been
growing at a rapid pace, with annual GDP growth rates averaging 6-7% over the past decade. Its
technology sector, particularly in IT services, is a global leader, with companies like Infosys and
Tata Consultancy Services shaping the future of the digital economy.
India’s geopolitical influence is also on the rise. As a major regional player in South Asia, India
exerts influence over neighboring countries and plays a key role in regional organisations like the
South Asian Association for Regional Cooperation (SAARC). India’s military is the fourth largest
in the world, with significant modernisation efforts underway, including advancements in space
technology and defense manufacturing. Additionally, India is an active participant in international
diplomacy through the United Nations, G20, and as a founding member of the BRICS.
Case Study 2: Saudi Arabia
Saudi Arabia is considered an emerging power due to its significant economic influence, driven
by vast oil reserves, and its growing regional and global political role.
As the world’s largest oil exporter, Saudi Arabia holds approximately 17% of the world’s proven
petroleum reserves, making it a key player in the global energy market and giving it huge power
over the world’s biggest economies due to how it can influence oil prices. Its economy, the largest
in the Middle East, has a nominal GDP of around $1.1 trillion (2024). Under its Vision 2030
initiative, Saudi Arabia has been making efforts to diversify its economy away from oil
dependency by investing in sectors such as tourism, entertainment, and technology.
Saudi Arabia’s geopolitical influence extends throughout the Middle East and beyond, as it plays
a leading role in the Gulf Cooperation Council (GCC) and the Organisation of the Petroleum
Exporting Countries (OPEC). It is also a key regional player in maintaining stability and balancing
the influence of countries like Iran. Saudi Arabia has made strategic alliances with Western
countries, particularly the United States, while also increasingly engaging with rising powers such
as China and India. The Kingdom’s military, which ranks among the top 10 global defense
spenders with a budget of around $75 billion (2023), is highly modernised and plays a central
role in regional security, particularly in conflicts such as the Yemen Civil War.
