Case Study - The United States as a Post-Cold War Superpower
Following the end of the Cold War in 1991, the United States emerged as the world’s sole
superpower. With the collapse of the Soviet Union, the U.S. found itself with unmatched global
influence, leading the world in military, economic, and cultural domains.
1. Military dominance: The U.S. military is the largest and most technologically advanced in the
world, with a defence budget of $842 billion (2024), 40% of the world’s total military spending and
more than the combined military spending of the next several countries. The U.S. maintains
hundreds of military bases across the globe, which allows it to project power in every region. Its
military capabilities include a vast nuclear arsenal, state-of-the-art missile defense systems, and
dominance in cyber warfare. The United States has been involved in various conflicts since the
Cold War, including the Gulf War, interventions in the Balkans, and the War on Terror in
Afghanistan and Iraq, which further demonstrated its capacity for global military intervention.
2. Economic dominance: The U.S. economy remains the largest in the world, with a nominal
GDP of over $28.78 trillion (2024). It has unparalleled influence over global financial institutions
like the International Monetary Fund (IMF) and World Bank and continues to lead global trade,
innovation, and investment. The U.S. dollar is the world’s dominant reserve currency, accounting
for around 60% of global foreign exchange reserves, giving the U.S. significant leverage over
international trade and finance.
3. Political and diplomatic power: The United States plays a leading role in many international
organisations, including the United Nations where it has a veto on the Security Council, NATO,
and the G7, and has been instrumental in shaping international law, human rights, and global
security. Through its diplomatic influence, the U.S. often acts as a mediator in global conflicts,
pushing for international resolutions and peace agreements.
4. Soft power: U.S. cultural exports, from Hollywood movies to technology giants like Google,
Apple, and Facebook, permeate global culture and communication. ‘American values’, including
democracy, freedom of speech, and free markets, have also been promoted through its foreign
policy, often shaping global governance and public opinion. The U.S. leads in technological
innovation and education, being home to many of the world’s largest tech companies and the
best universities, which bolsters its global influence.
Despite the rise of other powers, particularly China, the United States has maintained its
superpower status through its combination of military strength, economic dominance, political
leadership, and cultural influence. However, recent challenges—such as growing internal political
divisions, economic inequality, and the rise of China as a competitor—have sparked debates
about whether the US is in decline as a superpower. Nonetheless, its unparalleled military reach,
economic power, and cultural influence ensure its continued predominant role in global affairs.
China In Focus
The Rise Of China
China’s rise to prominence on the global stage has been one of the most significant geopolitical
developments of the 21st century. Over the past four decades, China has transformed from an
isolated, agrarian society into the world’s second-largest economy and a formidable global
power. This was driven by rapid industrialisation and urbanisation that led it to grow at an average
annual GDP rate of about 10% for much of this period.
The Pre-2008 Era: Economic Reforms and Opening Up:
China’s journey to global prominence began with economic reforms under Deng Xiaoping in 1978.
The shift away from a planned economy towards market-oriented policies initiated China’s rapid
economic growth. Deng’s policies focused on opening China to foreign investment, encouraging
https://www.politicsexplained.co.uk/ 9
privatization of industries, and developing China’s manufacturing sector. Special Economic Zones
(SEZs) like Shenzhen became symbols of China’s modernisation, attracting foreign capital and
driving exports. China’s economy grew from relative insignificance into one of the most important in
the world, but its global influence was primarily economic rather than geopolitical.
Throughout the 1990s and early 2000s, China continued to integrate into the global economy. The
most significant milestone came in 2001 when China joined the World Trade Organisation
(WTO), which boosted its international trade exponentially. By 2008, China had become the
world’s third-largest economy and a major player in global manufacturing. During this pre-2008
period, China maintained a low-profile foreign policy, focused on economic development rather
than geopolitical assertiveness, adhering to Deng’s philosophy of “hide your strength, bide
your time.”
Post-2008: China’s Transition to Global Power:
China’s rise took a significant turn after the 2008 global financial crisis, which weakened many
Western economies, particularly the United States and the European Union. In contrast, China
emerged relatively unscathed, maintaining robust growth and solidifying its position as the world’s
second-largest economy by 2010. The financial crisis marked a turning point, as China began to
play a more active role in global governance and adopted a more assertive foreign policy.
This is particularly evident under President Xi Jinping, who assumed power in 2012. Xi’s
leadership has been characterised by efforts to project China as a global leader, not only in
economic terms but also militarily and diplomatically. One key post-2008 initiative was the Belt
and Road Initiative (BRI), launched in 2013. The BRI aimed to extend China’s influence across
Asia, Africa, and Europe through infrastructure projects, trade partnerships, and investments.
China’s military has expanded significantly, with increased spending on modernising the People’s
Liberation Army (PLA), including its navy and missile capabilities. In the South China Sea, China
has engaged in the construction of artificial islands, asserting territorial claims that have brought it
into conflict with neighboring countries and the United States. China’s militarisation of this region
and its growing global military presence have been key indicators of its rising power. Xi Jinping’s
vision is of the ‘Rejuvenation Of The Chinese Nation’ that takes its place as a leading world power,
challenging U.S. dominance in many areas.
