The Belt and Road Initiative (BRI)
The Belt and Road Initiative (BRI), launched by Chinese President Xi Jinping in 2013, represents
one of the most ambitious infrastructure and investment projects in modern history. The BRI aims
to develop trade routes that link China with countries across Asia, Africa, Europe, and beyond,
reviving the ancient Silk Road trade routes. By 2024, over 140 countries have joined the initiative,
which involves a vast array of infrastructure projects, including the construction of roads,
railways, ports, energy pipelines, and communication networks. The initiative is designed to
enhance global trade, deepen China’s economic ties with partner countries, and extend
China’s strategic influence.
The BRI is divided into two major components: the Silk Road Economic Belt, which focuses on
connecting China to Central Asia and Europe via overland routes, and the 21st Century Maritime
Silk Road, which seeks to enhance maritime connectivity across Southeast Asia, the Indian
Ocean, and beyond. Some of the key projects include the China-Pakistan Economic Corridor
(CPEC), which involves constructing highways and energy projects between China and Pakistan,
and various port development projects in countries like Sri Lanka, Kenya, and Greece. These
will help China reduce its reliance on trade moving through the South China Sea and help
it secure an alternative route for oil from the Middle East.
Beyond infrastructure, the BRI also includes efforts to strengthen economic ties and promote
industrial cooperation among participating nations. China offers loans and investments to
developing countries to finance BRI projects, but this has led to criticism, with some
accusing China of engaging in “debt-trap diplomacy.” In cases such as Sri Lanka’s
Hambantota Port, where Sri Lanka struggled to repay loans, China gained control of strategic
assets, heightening concerns about Beijing’s geopolitical and military ambitions, as it could use
these ports for military purposes in the future.
China has used the BRI to extend its diplomatic and strategic reach into regions where Western
influence has traditionally been stronger. Africa, in particular, has been a major focus, with
countries like Kenya, Ethiopia, and Nigeria receiving significant Chinese investment through BRI
projects. Additionally, the initiative has further solidified China’s leadership in global
infrastructure development and its role in shaping the global economy, allowing China to
position itself as a crucial player in the economic development of participating nations.
Made In China 2025
The Made in China 2025 initiative, introduced in 2015, reflects China’s ambitions to
transform itself from a manufacturing giant of low-cost goods into a leader in high-tech
industries. The plan focuses on developing Chinese capabilities in key sectors such as artificial
intelligence, robotics, biotechnology, and aerospace, with the goal of reducing dependence on
foreign technology and positioning China as a global innovator and advanced economy that can
challenge the US, China and Germany.
By investing heavily in research and development (R&D), China aims to become self-sufficient in
critical technologies such as semiconductors and telecommunications, reducing its reliance on
the West. Major companies like Huawei and Tencent have become global leaders in technology,
driving the digital economy. This push has led to increased tensions with the United States and
other Western powers, who view China’s technological rise as a direct challenge to their global
dominance in innovation.
However, Made in China 2025 has also drawn international criticism, particularly from the U.S.,
which has accused China of engaging in unfair state subsidies & investment and intellectual
property theft to achieve its technological goals. Despite these challenges, China is rapidly
closing the gap in critical areas of technology, positioning itself as a potential future leader in
innovation.
