Is The United States A Declining Superpower
Arguments That The US Is A Decling Superpower
After three decades of effective unipolarity since the end of the Cold War, many political
commentators are speaking of the United States’ relative decline as a superpower.
● Economically: while the U.S. remains the largest economy in the world with a GDP of $26
trillion, its national debt has surpassed $33 trillion, raising concerns about the sustainability
of its fiscal policies. Income inequality continues to rise, with the top 1% of Americans
controlling nearly 40% of the nation’s wealth, leading to social and political tensions
domestically. The middle class, once a pillar of American economic strength, has seen
wages stagnate, and manufacturing jobs have been offshored, particularly to China, which
has rapidly become the world’s manufacturing hub. In fact, China is now the largest trading
partner for over 120 countries, compared to the U.S.’s shrinking share of global trade.
● Militarily: although the U.S. maintains the world’s largest defense budget at $877 billion
and continues to lead global alliances such as NATO, its strategic effectiveness has been
questioned. The U.S. withdrawal from Afghanistan in 2021, after nearly two decades of
costly military engagement, damaged American credibility and raised doubts about the
sustainability of its global military commitments. Meanwhile, China’s military rise,
particularly in the South China Sea and its investment in hypersonic missile technology,
challenges U.S. dominance, especially in the Indo-Pacific.
● Geopolitically: the U.S. faces increasing competition from China and Russia, both of which
are actively working to challenge the U.S.-led international order. China’s Belt and Road
Initiative (BRI) has provided Beijing with substantial influence over global infrastructure
development. Russia’s aggression in Ukraine and its alliance with China are further
reshaping global alliances, moving away from U.S. influence. At the same time, U.S.
relations with traditional allies have been strained in recent years, particularly during the
Trump administrations, which have pursued an isolationist “America First” policy that
alienated many in Europe and Asia. Although Biden has worked to rebuild these
relationships, the lingering effects of that period led some to question the U.S.’s long-term
reliability as a global leader and this was once again reinforced after Trump’s reelection.
● Domestically: the U.S. is grappling with deepening political polarisation and domestic
instability. The January 6th Capitol riot in 2021 exposed severe divisions within American
society and raised concerns about the durability of its democratic institutions. Partisan
gridlock in Congress has impeded efforts to address pressing issues like climate change,
healthcare, and infrastructure. These internal conflicts not only weaken the U.S.’s ability to
respond to global challenges but also tarnish its image as a model of democracy, reducing
its soft power on the international stage.
Arguments That The US Isn’t A Decling Superpower
Despite these challenges, the United States continues to hold unparalleled advantages in many
key areas that affirm its status as the world’s leading superpower.
